Overview

I went back to the basics to refresh my memory and strengthen my understanding of essential Linux commands, file operations, and shell behaviour.

As I continue my cybersecurity journey, I believe revisiting foundational concepts is just as important as learning new techniques.

Commands and Concepts Practised

1. "ls" — Listing Files

Used to inspect the current working directory and identify available files.

2. ">" — Output Redirection

Redirects standard output into a file. It can create a new file or overwrite an existing file's contents.

3. "cat" — Viewing File Contents

Used to display the contents of a file directly in the terminal and verify that the expected text was saved.

4. Command Syntax and Spacing

Linux commands must be entered with the correct syntax. For example, "echo learning" runs the "echo" command with an argument, whereas "echolearning" is interpreted as a different command name.

5. Special Characters and Quoting

I revisited how Bash handles special characters, particularly the exclamation mark ("!"), which can trigger history expansion when enabled. Single quotes can preserve literal text without triggering this expansion.

Practical Workflow

- Inspected the current directory using "ls".
- Used output redirection (">") to write text into "practice.txt".
- Used "cat" to verify the saved contents.
- Reviewed command spacing and shell quoting behaviour.

Key Takeaway

Going back to the basics is not going backwards. It is an opportunity to reinforce knowledge, identify gaps, and build confidence in the skills I will rely on as I progress in cybersecurity.

I'm committed to understanding the commands I use rather than simply memorising them.

